"""Supervisor report with explicit proposed research, prior evidence and references."""
from pathlib import Path
import re
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
source=(R/'scripts/hybrid/build_work_summary.py').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
exec(source.split("d.add_heading('Learning from Distracted Offline Data'")[0])
O=R/'reports/research_plan_summary';O.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
title='Investigating Information Gating and Domain Adversarial Alignment for Offline Visual Control Using Only Distracted Data'
d.add_heading(title,0)
d.add_heading('Research Plan and Progress Summary',1)
p('Prepared by\nWasi alsafa\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering\nRajshahi University of Engineering & Technology\nRajshahi, Bangladesh\n5 November 2026')
p('Paper co-author: Sadad Kadery')
chapters=['1 Research Problem and Proposed Objectives','2 Reinforcement Learning Background and Learning Progress','3 Literature Review and Research Motivation','4 Completed Training and Validation Stages','5 Methodology and Block Diagram','6 Main Results and Research Implications','7 Planned Experiments and Expected Outputs','References']
chapter('Contents')
for page,name in enumerate(chapters,3):p(f'{name} ................................ {page}')
chapter(chapters[0])
h('Research Problem')
p('This research investigates whether an agent can learn useful control behaviour from recorded images that contain visual distractions, without using clean observations for training. The initial task is cheetah-run. Images contain information about the animal and its movement, together with background videos, colour changes and camera variation. A model can learn these visual details as shortcuts and then perform poorly when the appearance changes [1], [3], [11].')
h('Proposed Research Direction')
p('I plan to investigate how learned information gating and domain-adversarial alignment interact. Gating limits the image information passed to an encoder. Alignment discourages features that reveal the visual domain. Inverse action prediction encourages features that explain recorded actions. The aim is to establish when these objectives support control, when they interfere, and whether a more reliable way of preserving action information is needed [3], [6], [7], [9].')
h('Main Research Question')
p('Does action-guided information gating help preserve useful control information during domain-adversarial alignment when training uses only distracted offline data?')
h('Specific Objectives')
for s in ['Establish matched baselines with and without gating and alignment, keeping the data split, action objective and update budget fixed.','Measure both control performance and the information retained by learned features, using independent action and domain probes.','Investigate the causes of failure through alignment-strength, mask and inference controls before proposing a new mechanism.','Test whether findings repeat across more training seeds, evaluation conditions and, later, additional control tasks.']:p('• '+s)
h('Scope and Current Evidence')
p('The main completed study uses easy and medium distracted data as two visual domains. It does not use a clean training dataset. This differs from the earlier easy-only pilot. The controlled study is offline imitation with inverse representation learning and behavioural cloning, without a reward-learning critic. It is not a complete reproduction of the original domain-adversarial RL algorithm. The primary negative result is a reason to investigate the interaction more carefully, rather than claim an improved algorithm.')
chapter(chapters[1])
h('Core Reinforcement Learning Concepts')
p('In reinforcement learning, an agent observes an environment, chooses an action, receives a reward and observes the outcome. A policy chooses actions. A value function estimates future reward; a critic learns such estimates. An episode is one sequence from reset to termination or a time limit. Deep RL uses neural networks for policies, value functions or image encoders.')
p('The objective is to obtain high expected cumulative reward. A common discounted return is G = r₀ + γr₁ + γ²r₂ + …, where γ controls how strongly future rewards count. A single image may hide velocity, so several recent frames are stacked. An encoder converts these images into compact features for later prediction or action selection.')
h('Offline RL and Behavioural Cloning')
p('Online RL collects new experience while learning. Offline RL learns from a fixed dataset of observations, actions, rewards and subsequent observations. A replay buffer samples records from this dataset. Limited action coverage can make reward estimates unreliable outside the recorded behaviour. Behavioural cloning is a simpler offline learning rule that copies recorded actions; it does not itself learn a reward-based critic. BC can also be used as regularization within offline RL algorithms [1], [12].')
table(['Term','Meaning in our experiments'],[['Transition','One recorded environment step, not a complete game.'],['Training episode','A sequence of 500 transitions in the selected benchmark data.'],['Training seed','Controls initialization and sampling; separate seeds produce separate trained agents.'],['Evaluation seed','Controls a fresh simulator rollout and its random settings.'],['Update','An optimization event on a sampled batch; data can be reused many times.'],['Return','The sum of rewards in an evaluation episode; higher is better on cheetah-run.']])
h('Learning Progress')
p('I followed a tutorial to build a Snake-game RL agent and began the UC Berkeley deep RL course on YouTube. This introduced the observation–action–reward loop and repeated training. My continuing study covers image encoders, actor–critic methods, replay sampling, loss functions, gradients, behavioural cloning, experimental controls and checkpointing. The course is ongoing. These learning exercises support understanding of the research implementation; they are not research results by themselves.')
chapter(chapters[2])
table(['Source','Relevance to the proposed work'],[['V-D4RL [1] and DrQ-v2 [2]','Visual offline benchmarks and an efficient visual actor–critic foundation. An offline adaptation must be distinguished from original online DrQ-v2.'],['Domain-adversarial visual offline RL [3]','Uses a discriminator, reversed gradients and DropBlock to reduce dependence on domain-specific appearance. Its main setup uses normal and distracted data; it also discusses two distracted datasets.'],['SeMOPO [4]','Separates agent-related and external latent factors to avoid misleading world-model uncertainty. No world model was trained in our study.'],['Synthetic Data [5]','Uses augmentation and diffusion-generated latent data for generalization. Our RGB pilot is a much simpler control.'],['ACRO [6]','Uses multi-step inverse prediction to encourage agent-related representations. Its guarantees depend on assumptions; they do not automatically apply to our adaptation.'],['InfoGating [7]','Learns to restrict information while supporting a task objective. We use adapted author encoder, gate and inverse-model components.'],['MMCS [8]','Related latent masking and sufficiency objectives. Author code and accessible publisher material were inspected; the full paywalled paper was not obtained.'],['Gradient reversal [9] and DCS [11]','Provide the alignment principle and background/camera/colour distraction convention.']])
h('What Remains Open in This Project')
p('Using distracted data alone is not sufficient novelty, because prior work already considers it [3]. Nor does placing two existing methods together establish a contribution. The proposed contribution is evidence about their interaction under matched conditions, followed by a targeted change only if a repeatable failure is established.')
p('The key tension is that alignment may remove features that also help action prediction, while a sparse gate may discard useful information or retain a shortcut. A successful preservation claim requires both reliable control performance and supporting representation tests. A visually plausible mask or a low training loss is insufficient.')
chapter(chapters[3])
table(['Stage','Recorded setup','Purpose and outcome'],[['1 Pipeline smoke test','10,000 distracted transitions; batch 16; 200 optimizer-update events.','Completed 10 October 2026. All 20 recorded acceptance checks passed. Verified end-to-end operation, not policy quality.'],['2 Six-method pilot','100,000 transitions from 200 complete easy-distraction episodes; three seeds/method; 1,200 update seconds/run.','Compared BC, DrQv2+BC, RGB augmentation, two-view policy consistency, fixed masking and adapted InfoGating.'],['3 Controlled study','160,000 training transitions from easy and medium domains; four conditions × three seeds; 40,000 updates/run.','All 12 runs completed. Tested gating and alignment with a shared inverse objective and BC policy.']])
h('First Stage Validation')
p('The smoke test checked data fields, episode boundaries, frame stacking, GPU computation, finite losses, bounded actions, parameter changes and checkpoint reload. Simulator rendering and evaluation were tested without adding evaluation transitions to the training buffer. This established a usable pipeline, but 200 update events cannot demonstrate convergence or distraction robustness.')
h('Second Stage Findings')
table(['Method','Held-out return mean ± SD'],[['BC','7.68 ± 5.99'],['DrQv2+BC','1.81 ± 0.70'],['Stronger RGB augmentation','19.63 ± 14.91'],['Two-view policy consistency','9.98 ± 11.78'],['Fixed top-band mask','6.26 ± 5.77'],['Adapted InfoGating','59.74 ± 57.04']])
p('Adapted InfoGating had the highest mean but very large variation. Architectures and objectives differed, and equal update time did not mean equal updates. This motivated the controlled study; it did not prove a gating advantage. The second stage also included short 10,000/100,000-transition data-size pilots and central-band occlusion tests. Results were mixed. In total, it comprised 30 runs, about 7.0001 measured update hours and 234 evaluations. Its scores must not be pooled with the later protocol.')
chapter(chapters[4])
p('All conditions share inverse action prediction and a behavioural cloning policy. The learned gate and reversed alignment signal are switched on only for the relevant conditions. This isolates their interaction more clearly than the earlier six-method pilot.')
d.add_picture(str(R/'experiments/hybrid_20261030/ieee_template/figures/methodology.png'),width=Inches(4.1))
p('Figure 1. Implemented training and evaluation pipeline. The policy receives detached encoder features, so its cloning loss updates the policy only. Alignment conditions reverse the representation gradient; evaluation uses raw images without model updates.')
table(['Condition','Gate','Alignment'],[['Inv','No','No'],['IG','Yes','No'],['DA+Inv','No','Yes'],['DA+IG','Yes','Yes']])
chapter(chapters[5])
h('Data Separation and Training Budget')
p('For each easy/medium source shard, 25 complete episodes were selected and split into 20 training, 3 probe-fit and 2 probe-test episodes. This yielded 160,000 training, 24,000 probe-fit and 16,000 probe-test transitions. Batches contained eight samples from each domain. All 12 models reached 40,000 updates, using about 6.376 measured update hours on a GTX 1650. Loading, evaluation and checkpoint time are excluded.')
table(['Condition','Primary held-out return','Action probe MSE'],[['Inv','142.36 ± 45.05','0.1518'],['IG','129.18 ± 8.81','0.1570'],['DA+Inv','157.73 ± 38.63','0.1499'],['DA+IG','109.61 ± 9.51','0.1539']])
p('Each final seed mean averages five simulator episodes; reported variation is sample SD across three training seeds. Adding gating to alignment reduced primary held-out return in all matched seeds: mean paired change −48.11, SD 29.13. The combined model also had higher action probe error than alignment alone. Every raw-feature domain probe obtained accuracy 1.0, versus balanced chance 0.5. Domain information therefore remained linearly recoverable.')
h('Secondary Test and Interpretation')
p('A separate 120-episode fresh-seed test jointly changed background, camera and colour. DA+IG obtained moving-distraction return 79.05 ± 4.05, compared with 73.18 ± 19.81 for DA+Inv. The mean ranking reversed, and even the static scores changed with the fresh seeds. This makes evaluation coverage a priority; it does not establish a reliable moving-distraction advantage or identify the cause of the differences.')
h('Reliability and Limits')
p('Data audits checked 48 split files, episode boundaries, finite values, action bounds and hashes. Exact action-sequence comparisons found no identical cross-split sequences, but approximately related physical trajectories could remain. Independent probes fit separate episodes and test separate episodes; sampled pairs can repeat. Checkpoints were saved every 2,500 updates. A restart interrupted probes after training had finished; missing probes were resumed without retraining completed models.')
p('The current evidence is limited to one task, three seeds and an adapted BC representation learner. It does not establish domain invariance, a superior RL algorithm or a full reproduction of [3]. More informative diagnostics and broader testing are needed before making stronger claims.')
chapter(chapters[6])
table(['Planned step','Controlled comparison','Evidence sought'],[['1 Check repeatability','More independent training/evaluation seeds with fixed budgets and predeclared endpoints.','Whether the direction survives broader evaluation; more seeds do not guarantee significance.'],['2 Diagnose alignment','Sweep alignment weight and classifier capacity while holding other settings fixed.','Whether independently fitted domain probes become less accurate without losing action information.'],['3 Diagnose the gate','Inspect retention maps; compare gate penalties and raw/gated inference in separately labelled tests.','Whether important regions are suppressed or training/inference differences contribute to failure.'],['4 Test mask and view controls','Fixed masks, imperfect masks and two-view consistency, with matched objectives.','Whether a simpler mechanism explains any benefit and how useful-region removal affects control.'],['5 Restore reward-learning comparison','Implement or rerun a faithful original-method baseline under a matched protocol.','Whether findings carry from offline imitation to reward-based offline RL.'],['6 Broaden the study','Additional locomotion tasks, data sizes and isolated distraction types.','Whether results generalize beyond cheetah and one appearance split.']])
h('Expected Outputs and Decision Rule')
p('The planned outputs are reproducible configurations, checkpoints, evaluation records, mask diagnostics, feature-probe results and a revised thesis/paper analysis. A new preservation mechanism will be proposed only after a repeatable failure is identified. It must be compared with matched action-supervised and ungated controls. Unsuccessful conditions and compute costs will be retained.')
h('Manuscript and Project Status')
p('A five-page IEEE-style manuscript and an anonymous review copy have been prepared, including the methodology diagram and actual findings. Submission has been reported during project discussions; acceptance or publication is not established here. The code and small records are available in the project repository [10]. Large data, environments and checkpoints are excluded from ordinary Git history. Upstream attribution is preserved; external source with unresolved licence terms is not redistributed.')
p('AI assistance supported implementation, experiment orchestration, analysis and drafting. The reported numerical results come from saved experiments. Continuing coursework and independent review of the code remain part of the research work. A simulator demonstration may help communicate the findings, but videos will accompany systematic evaluation rather than replace it.')
